\end{align}

If $G=\operatorname{GL}_r$, and take $\mu$ to be 
\begin{align*}
    \mu=(\overbrace{1, \ldots, 1}^{m}, 0, \ldots, 0) \in \mathbb{Z}^r
\end{align*}
    for $0\leqslant m\leqslant r$. It is minuscule coweight, and the fibers of the maps in (\ref{Hecke:dia:pq}) are Grassmannians $\mathrm{Gr}(r, m)$.
In this case, we can describe the Hecke functor $H_x^{\mu}$ in terms of a right action 
of an object $A_m \in \mathbb{T}(m)_0$, defined in (\ref{Am}) as follows. 

We have the following (non-Cartesian) diagram 
\begin{align*}
\xymatrix{
\mathfrak{gl}_m/\operatorname{GL}_m \ar@<-0.3ex>@{^{(}->}[r]^-{i} \ar[d] & \mathcal{S}(m) \ar[d] \\
B\operatorname{GL}_m \ar@<-0.3ex>@{^{(}->}[r] & \mathcal{C}(m). 
}
    \end{align*}
Here 
the bottom horizontal arrow is the closed immersion corresponding to the closed point 
$F=\mathcal{O}_x^{\oplus m}$, and the top arrow corresponds 
to the Higgs field 
$\operatorname{Hom}(F, F\otimes \omega_C)=\mathfrak{gl}_m$. 
We set 
\begin{align}\label{Am}
    A_m :=i_{\ast}\mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{gl}_m/\operatorname{GL}_m}[-mr] \in \operatorname{Coh}(\mathcal{S}(m)). 
\end{align}
It is straightforward to check that the functor $H_x^{\mu}$ is given by 
